WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.570
I think there are many reasons why
people notice certain things better

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.360
than other people, particularly
when it comes to pronunciation.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.960
But I'm sure the same is true in
terms of noticing things in grammar.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.730
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.970
Today I want to talk about
noticing in language learning.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.740
Uh, there's a thing called the noticing
hypothesis, which has created some

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:25.050
controversy and in linguistic circles,
but I'm going to talk about how noticing

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.910
influences our ability to learn language.

00:00:27.555 --> 00:00:30.315
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:30.345 --> 00:00:32.625
You can click on the
bell for notifications.

00:00:32.955 --> 00:00:38.445
So I have said before that one of the
best descriptions of language learning of

00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:43.035
what is involved in learning a language
is something I heard at a conference of

00:00:43.394 --> 00:00:49.185
language teachers in San Diego where,
um, one of the people presenting there

00:00:49.245 --> 00:00:53.295
said language learning consists of three
things: the attitude of the learner,

00:00:54.195 --> 00:00:58.905
the time spent with the language, not
listening to explanations in English

00:00:58.905 --> 00:01:03.315
or something, uh, so time spent with
a language, attitude, time spent with

00:01:03.315 --> 00:01:05.685
the language and the ability to notice.

00:01:06.645 --> 00:01:11.655
Now I notice that I notice things
when I'm learning a language and

00:01:11.655 --> 00:01:13.305
I notice them at different times.

00:01:13.875 --> 00:01:18.075
Now, apparently there is a thing
called the noticing hypothesis, which

00:01:18.075 --> 00:01:21.285
was proposed in 1960 by a professor.

00:01:21.885 --> 00:01:26.805
And again, as with so much in the sort of
academic approach to language learning, he

00:01:26.815 --> 00:01:32.295
sort of, as I can understand it, trying to
read the explanations online of noticing

00:01:32.295 --> 00:01:37.835
hypothesis, he sort of presents a somewhat
extreme case of the importance of noticing

00:01:38.115 --> 00:01:44.115
to language acquisition, and therefore
it became sort of in opposition to, or in

00:01:44.115 --> 00:01:48.854
some ways in contradiction to what Krashen
said about the importance of input, sort

00:01:48.854 --> 00:01:50.835
of just naturally absorbing the language.

00:01:51.255 --> 00:01:54.854
You know, he implies that you have
to kind of deliberately notice

00:01:54.854 --> 00:01:57.810
things to be able to acquire them.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.060
And, uh, so I, you know, I, I invite
you to Google for it, uh, Wikipedia

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.630
or elsewhere, and see if you can make
sense of this linguistics debate.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:10.680
I can't really, but I would
like to talk about noticing.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.720
My impression of the importance
of noticing to language learning.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:21.900
First of all, when we begin with a
new language, we don't notice much.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.420
It's all noise to us.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.140
Like if you're trying to notice
the pronunciation, you can't

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.489
really hear it that well.

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:34.620
And so everything is kind of like
a blank slate, but certain things

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.560
start to fall into place naturally.

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:42.299
And as we, and so that we
start to notice certain things.

00:02:42.299 --> 00:02:47.160
We notice that, uh, you know, how
certain things are pronounced and some

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.290
people may notice better than others.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:51.240
But that's...

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:57.359
we notice more than other things that
had previously been hidden come forward.

00:02:57.359 --> 00:03:01.359
And we start to notice
them a bit like a jigsaw.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.990
So when you start a jigsaw
puzzle, it's totally blank.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:09.600
As you start to put some of the pieces of
the jigsaw puzzle into place, you start to

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.420
see, you know, greater likelihood of where
some of the pieces might fit and, and

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.920
language learning is very much like that.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.380
So you start to notice that
there's a gap here, that maybe

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.329
this is where this piece belongs.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:26.630
And, uh, certainly that's been my
experience, uh, you know, in Russian,

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:33.260
for example, um, you, you know, any
un, um, accentuated syllable that

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.739
has the letter "o", what we would
call the sound "o" in it becomes

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.790
"a", you don't notice that at first.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:43.395
Uh, you know, and then eventually
you start to notice that, and once

00:03:43.395 --> 00:03:46.125
you've noticed that, now you're
ready to notice something else.

00:03:46.495 --> 00:03:50.295
And I don't know that there's any
special order for these things,

00:03:50.595 --> 00:03:54.855
except to say that the more things
we have already noticed, the more

00:03:55.515 --> 00:03:57.525
new things we can start to notice.

00:03:57.585 --> 00:04:01.425
Now, is it a deliberate
activity, that of noticing?

00:04:01.785 --> 00:04:02.235
Okay.

00:04:02.565 --> 00:04:07.739
I think first of all, the more we
listen and read the more we naturally

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:12.149
almost subconsciously notice things
because we're naturally, this is sort

00:04:12.149 --> 00:04:17.310
of Krashen's input hypothesis, we're
just gradually acquiring the language.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:23.280
Our brain is starting to recognize certain
patterns in the language, so that even

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.479
without deliberately trying to notice
anything, we start to notice things.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:27.960
All right.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:33.900
Uh, and, but I had the experience
as I listened to a mini story for

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:39.119
the 30th time, I noticed something
that I hadn't noticed before.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:43.140
So we are continuing to notice
just through this process

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.109
of listening and reading.

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:49.669
However, okay, and some people
notice better than others.

00:04:49.969 --> 00:04:53.880
Uh, if, if someone pronounces
something in a foreign language, I

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.300
will tend to notice that pronunciation,
to pick up on it right away.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:57.590
Right.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:02.120
And I've said, you know, uh, when I was
in Japan and I'd have visitors from north

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.500
America who would be introduced to Mr.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.160
Sato, people would  say Mr.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.730
Sato, because it's written S A T O.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.730
So they see the name card S A T O.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.490
That should be Sato.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.380
Uh, even though they hear
him pronounce his own name.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.840
So they don't notice the pronunciation.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.480
Other people will immediately
hear Sato and go Sato.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:29.340
So, uh, the ability to
notice varies with people.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.950
Now, are there psychological reasons why
some people notice better than others?

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:38.580
Some people are more willing
to just go with what they hear,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.630
whereas other people are more stuck
with the way the word is written.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.880
We have all kinds of examples of people.

00:05:45.705 --> 00:05:50.955
You know, my father who was from
Czechoslovakia "word" was "ward"

00:05:51.165 --> 00:05:54.975
W O R D because to him, that's
the way it should be pronounced.

00:05:55.005 --> 00:05:56.025
That's how it's written.

00:05:56.265 --> 00:05:59.325
So there was a certain resistance
to the fact that in English

00:05:59.355 --> 00:06:02.085
actually it's pronounced "word"
is not pronounced "ward".

00:06:03.105 --> 00:06:07.905
Um, so I think there are many reasons
why people notice certain things

00:06:07.935 --> 00:06:11.265
better than other people, particularly
when it comes to pronunciation.

00:06:11.265 --> 00:06:14.895
But I'm sure the same is true in
terms of noticing things in grammar.

00:06:14.924 --> 00:06:16.604
Now, what can help us notice?

00:06:17.715 --> 00:06:19.395
First of all, we have to want to notice.

00:06:20.025 --> 00:06:24.635
And second of all obviously
a teacher or a grammatical

00:06:24.664 --> 00:06:26.465
explanation can help us notice.

00:06:26.465 --> 00:06:31.835
Like I have very often read an
explanation of something and then I

00:06:31.835 --> 00:06:35.854
start to notice it when I next see that
in a text, whether it be a structure

00:06:35.854 --> 00:06:40.145
or even some aspect of pronunciation,
however, we have to be ready.

00:06:40.905 --> 00:06:45.195
We have to be ready to receive
that bit of information, because

00:06:45.195 --> 00:06:49.665
if it comes too early, we don't
know enough about the language, we

00:06:49.665 --> 00:06:54.945
haven't experienced enough about the
language for that hint to be useful.

00:06:55.725 --> 00:06:59.835
So maybe having struggled with
Russian for a certain amount of time

00:06:59.835 --> 00:07:04.695
and, um, say I'm able to pronounce
certain things at that point.

00:07:04.695 --> 00:07:10.545
If someone points out that actually
yeah, an accentuated, uh, uh, you know,

00:07:10.575 --> 00:07:12.684
syllables with "o" are pronounced "a".

00:07:13.414 --> 00:07:18.044
Now I notice it, if that had been
told to me right up front at the

00:07:18.044 --> 00:07:21.435
beginning, I don't think I would
have been able to retain that.

00:07:22.125 --> 00:07:24.495
And I think I would have missed it
the next time and the next time,

00:07:24.525 --> 00:07:25.755
the next time that it showed up.

00:07:25.965 --> 00:07:28.215
So there is a question of timing.

00:07:28.484 --> 00:07:32.445
And so far as noticing is concerned
timing both in terms of what you're

00:07:32.445 --> 00:07:37.575
going to naturally notice and also in
terms of what, you know, teachers or

00:07:37.575 --> 00:07:43.080
grammar resources or other explanations
can...in terms of how useful they

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.150
can be in helping you to notice.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:49.530
But I do feel that in order to,
and I guess this is where I agree

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.690
with aspects of the, uh, noticing
hypothesis, uh, you have to be able

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:58.800
to notice things first before you can
actually start to use them yourself.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.940
And, and we've all had the experience
that once we noticed something that

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:06.360
for the longest time we never noticed,
once we noticed it here, all of a

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:07.390
sudden you start to see it there.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:08.430
And when you see it there and we see...

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.860
and, uh, there is a term for that,
which I've forgotten, but once

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.800
certain things are either pointed
out to us or we, we become aware of

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:18.120
them and we start to notice them.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:19.409
Then we see them elsewhere.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:24.420
And I think noticing is very much a part
of your brain starting to, you know,

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:29.520
recognize patterns in the language so
that initially your comprehension improves

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:34.475
and eventually your ability to pronounce
things correctly or to, uh, you know,

00:08:34.505 --> 00:08:36.315
use the structure correctly will improve.

00:08:36.784 --> 00:08:39.515
So noticing is very much
a part of the process.

00:08:39.815 --> 00:08:45.905
I think the biggest contributor to our
ability to notice is a quantity of content

00:08:45.905 --> 00:08:48.245
that we consume, reading and listening.

00:08:48.905 --> 00:08:52.685
Another important factor is our
willingness to notice, our willingness

00:08:52.685 --> 00:08:57.215
to accept this, that, in other
words, we, we leave aside the

00:08:57.215 --> 00:08:59.064
prejudices from our own language.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.560
That the structure should be
this way, because this is the

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:02.970
way we say it in our language.

00:09:03.689 --> 00:09:07.350
This should be pronounced this way,
because that's how it's pronounced

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.830
in our language or that's how that
those are the values that those

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:12.930
letters have in our native language.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:17.460
So, you know, more willingness to
accept and, and also the desire

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.260
to want to hear what's going on.

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:23.880
So there is an element of wanting
to notice, and then there's also the

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:28.199
sort of external factors that can
help us notice such as a teacher,

00:09:28.199 --> 00:09:31.560
such as somebody pointing something
out, pointing out a mistake, for

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:36.060
example, uh, these are all part and
parcel of what helps us to notice.

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:40.350
And I think we have to be able to notice
in order to improve in a language.

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:46.440
So I strayed a bit from the sort of
pure noticing hypothesis, but those

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.760
are the ways I have noticed that
noticing helps me learn languages.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.040
So, uh, thank you for listening.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:53.670
Bye for now.

